<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Title>Domain Driven Design Toolkit - Postgres</Title>
    <PackageTags>ddd;domain;driven;design;toolkit;entity;framework;efcore;postgres;postgresql;rls;row-level-security;policies</PackageTags>
    <Description>Postgres row level security for the DDDToolkit: every connection a context opens runs under the caller's role and token claims, and the [RowAccess] rules written in C# become the policies Postgres enforces, on any Postgres, Supabase's included.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Entity Framework's relational layer, for the connection interceptor and for the model the policies
    are written from: which table an aggregate lives in and what its columns are called. It talks to
    Postgres through DbConnection and DbCommand rather than referencing Npgsql, so the application brings
    whichever Npgsql provider it already uses. The toolkit itself for the caller: who the application is
    acting for is not a Postgres question, and the hosts that answer it, the Auth packages, need no
    Entity Framework.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DDDToolkit\DDDToolkit.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    How a model marks a table that only grows, an event log for one, and which entity types are the outbox's
    and the inbox's. DDDToolkit.EntityFramework writes the marks; this package reads them, to write such a
    table's guard and privileges, and references that package no more than it references Npgsql. So the one
    file that spells the marks is compiled into both assemblies, as internal types.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\DDDToolkit.EntityFramework\Storage\KeptRows.cs" Link="Storage\KeptRows.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The hint an access guard of the database refuses with. DDDToolkit.EntityFramework reads it from a failed save
    and answers access.refused; this package writes it into every access guard it writes, and hands it to a guard
    of the application's own through RowAccessModel.Refusal. One file, compiled into both, as KeptRows is.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\DDDToolkit.EntityFramework\Interceptors\GuardMark.cs" Link="Interceptors\GuardMark.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
